[17592186152592 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363807 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186152592 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186152592 1071 "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $6$?\n\n1. $R_{5}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $5$\n2. $R^{5}$\n3. $M_{6}(R)$, the vector space of all $6 \\cdot 6$ matrices over $R$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186152592 1071 "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $6?$\n\n- ${R}_{5}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $5$\n- ${R}^{5}$\n- ${\\text{M}}_{6}(R),$ the vector space of all $6\\times 6$ matrices over $R$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186152592 1074 "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{5}[t])=5+1=6.\\,✓\n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}^{n})=n.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}^{5})=5\\ne 6.\\,\\times \n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n}(R))={n}^{2}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{6}(R))={6}^{2}=36\\ne 6.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186152625 1068 #edb/ref 17592186150227 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186152625 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363985 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186152625 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186152625 1071 "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-9t{y}^{′}+41y=0,\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1,\\,t>0.\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186152625 1071 "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$t^{2}y^{″} - 9ty^{′} + 41y = 0, y(1) = 1, y^{′}(1) = 1, t > 0$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186152625 1074 "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={t}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}-9t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}+41{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }-9\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }+41{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-9\\lambda +41] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-9\\lambda +41 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +41 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve the auxiliary equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +41 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +25)-25+41 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -5)^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda -5 & =\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =5\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =5\\pm 4\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{5+4\\text{i}} & ={t}^{5}{e}^{\\text{i}4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}={t}^{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)), \\\\ {t}^{5-4\\text{i}} & ={t}^{5}{e}^{-\\text{i}4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}={t}^{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =A{t}^{5+4\\text{i}}+B{t}^{5-4\\text{i}} \\\\  & =A{t}^{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))+B{t}^{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)) \\\\  & =(A+B){t}^{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\text{i}(A-B){t}^{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite the expression for $y(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny(t)={t}^{5}({c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1}=A+B$ and ${c}_{2}=\\text{i}(A-B).$  \n\nNow let's find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $y(1)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n1={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=1.$ First, we substitute ${c}_{1}=1$ and  differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & ={t}^{4}((5{c}_{2}-4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+(4{c}_{2}+5)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =4{c}_{2}+5 \\\\ -4 & =4{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t)={t}^{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)).\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186161811 1071 "Given that $A$ is a real symmetric matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ must have all real eigenvalues\n\n- $A$ is diagonalizable\n\n- If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are two eigenvectors, then $\\mathbf{u} \\perp \\mathbf{v}$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186161811 1071 "Given that $A$ is a real symmetric matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $A$ must have all real eigenvalues\n2. $A$ is diagonalizable\n3. If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are two eigenvectors, then $\\mathbf{u} \\perp \\mathbf{v}$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186161811 1074 "Given that $A$ is an $n \\times n$ symmetric matrix over $\\mathbb{R}$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the eigenvalues of a symmetric matrix are real.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable. Hence, it is diagonalizable.\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the theorem, eigenvectors corresponding to *different* eigenvalues must be orthogonal, but two eigenvectors corresponding to the *same* eigenvalue may not be orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186161811 1074 "Given that $A$ is an $n \\times n$ symmetric matrix over $\\mathbb{R}$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the eigenvalues of a symmetric matrix are real.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable. Hence, it is diagonalizable.\n- Statement III is false. According to the theorem, eigenvectors corresponding to *different* eigenvalues must be orthogonal, but two eigenvectors corresponding to the *same* eigenvalue may not be orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186161812 1071 "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $\\mathbf x\\in \\mathbb R^2$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-1$ of the matrix ![](images/55/554407e7ec109f5d6e767d87900321d07c253cceefd6e1cdd21ec11351c4548e.png)" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186161812 1071 "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $\\mathbf x\\in \\mathbb R^2$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-1$ of the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186161812 1074 "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\lambda_1 \\mathbf u_1\\cdot \\mathbf u_1^T + \\lambda_2 \\mathbf u_2\\cdot \\mathbf u_2^T\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u_1$ and $\\mathbf u_2$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$, respectively.\n\nMoreover, $\\mathbf u_i\\cdot \\mathbf u_i^T$ is the projection matrix corresponding to $\\mathbf u_i$.\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda=-1$. Computing $A-(-1)I$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - (-1)I &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} - (-1)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} + \\frac{1}{2}R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx_1=\\dfrac12x_2\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $x_2=2$, we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{v} \\| = \\sqrt{5}\n$$\n\nDividing $\\mathbf{v}$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu \\cdot u^{T} &= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begi