etric, then ${A}^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists)." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186162700 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. If $A$ is *any* $n \\cdot n$ matrix, then $A - A^{T}$ is always symmetric.\n2. The sum of two $n \\cdot n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric.\n3. If $A$ is symmetric, then $A^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists)." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186162700 1074 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nHowever, $A-{A}^{T}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{A}^{T} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any two symmetric matrices $A$ and $B,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)^{T} & ={A}^{T}+{B}^{T} \\\\  & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any non-singular symmetric matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}^{-1})^{T} & =({A}^{T})^{-1} \\\\  & ={A}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186166758 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362509 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186166758 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362523 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186166759 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364394 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186166759 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364401 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186166759 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364408 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186166900 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362570 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186166901 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362577 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186166901 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362584 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186166902 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363410 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186166903 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363417 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186166903 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364151 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186166904 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363427 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186166943 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363599 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186166943 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363610 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186166944 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363607 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186166944 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363615 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186166945 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363652 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186166945 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363665 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186166946 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363676 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186167191 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164141 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186167191 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164142 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186167191 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363406 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186167191 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363408 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186167191 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186167191 1071 "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]}},\\,Φ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & -3{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & 2{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n| ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | $-10{e}^{-2t}$ |  | $\\,,\\,$ | ${x}_{p}(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | $2$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |  |  |  |" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186167191 1071 "$x^{′}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x(t) + \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(f(t)), Φ = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{2t} & -3e^{4t} \\\\ 0 & 2e^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute $Φ^{-1}f(t)$ and the particular solution $x_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n| $Φ^{-1}f(t) =$ |  | $-10e^{-2t}$ |  | $$, | $x_{p}(t) =$ |  | $2$ |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |  |  |  |" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186167191 1074 "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & -3{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & 2{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{2t}\\cdot 2{e}^{4t}-(-3{e}^{4t})\\cdot 0}[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{4t} & 3{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-6t}}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{4t} & 3{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-2t} & 3{e}^{-2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-2t} & 3{e}^{-2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10{e}^{-2t} \\\\ -4{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}-10{e}^{-2t} \\\\ -4{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int -10{e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -4{e}^{-4t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & -3{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & 2{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186171840 1071 "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n![](images/3b/3bf74b3f4c11508b9daa66464cc451933f5637c22ffeaaae303378fca2571893.png)\nand the curve $\\mathcal C,$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathcal C:\\: 2x_1^2+6x_1x_2+ 2x_2^2 -2x_1+2x_2 =7 .\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(\\mathbf{x})=2x_1^2+6x_1x_2+2x_2^2$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $5y_1^2-y_2^2,$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $\\mathcal C?$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186171840 1071 "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand the curve $\\mathcal C$, defined as\n$\\mathcal C:\\: 2x_1^2+6x_1x_2+ 2x_2^2 -2x_1+2x_2 =7$.\n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(\\mathbf{x})=2x_1^2+6x_1x_2+2x_2^2$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $5y_1^2-y_2^2$, what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $\\mathcal C$?" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186171840 1074 "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\: \\underbrace{ 2x_1^2+6x_1x_2+2x_2^2}_{Q(\\mathbf{x})} -2x_1+2x_2 =7\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n![](images/78/783b32a7595534982e5fb057c049081564807b9d47988670c7d30058fb3900a0.png)\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(\\mathbf x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $\\mathcal C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} & =7 \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}-2(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2})+2(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2}) & =7 \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}+\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2} & =7 \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2} & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable $y_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2} & =7 \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}-({y}_{2}^{2}-2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2}) & =7 \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}-({y}_{2}^{2}-2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2})+2 & =7 \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}-({y}_{2}^{2}-2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2})+(\\sqrt{2})^{2} & =7 \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}-{({y}_{2}-\\sqrt{2})}^{2}+2 & =7 \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}-{({y}_{2}-\\sqrt{2})}^{2} & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}={y}_{1}, \\\\ {z}_{2}={y}_{2}-\\sqrt{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}_{1}^{2}-{({y}_{2}-\\sqrt{2})}^{2} & =5 \\\\ 5{z}_{1}^{2}-{z}_{2}^{2} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents a hyperbola with semiaxes $1$ and $\\sqrt5.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186171840 1074 "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\: \\underbrace{2x_1^2+6x_1x_2+2x_2^2}_{Q(\\mathbf{x})} -2x_1+2x_2 =7\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(\\mathbf x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $\\mathcal C$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x_{1}^{2} + 6x_{1}x_{2} + 2x_{2}^{2} - 2x_{1} + 2x_{2} &= 7 \\\\\n5y_{1}^{2} - y_{2}^{2} - 2(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}y_{1} - \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}y_{2}) + 2(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}y_{1} + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}y_{2}) &= 7 \\\\\n5y_{1}^{2} - y_{2}^{2} + \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}}y_{2} &= 7 \\\\\n5y_{1}^{2} - y_{2}^{2} + 2\\sqrt{2}y_{2} &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable $y_2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5y_{1}^{2} - y_{2}^{2} + 2\\sqrt{2}y_{2} &= 7 \\\\\n5y_{1}^{2} - (y_{2}^{2} - 2\\sqrt{2}y_{2}) &= 7 \\\\\n5y_{1}^{2} - (y_{2}^{2} - 2\\sqrt{2}y_{2} + (\\sqrt{2})^{2}) + 2 &= 7 \\\\\n5y_{1}^{2} - (y_{2}^{2} - 2\\sqrt{2}y_{2} + (\\sqrt{2})^{2}) + (\\sqrt{2})^{2} &= 7 \\\\\n5y_{1}^{2} - (y_{2} - \\sqrt{2})^{2} + 2 &= 7 \\\\\n5y_{1}^{2} - (y_{2} - \\sqrt{2})^{2} &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n{z_{1} = y_{1},; z_{2} = y_{2} - \\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5y_{1}^{2} - (y_{2} - \\sqrt{2})^{2} &= 5 \\\\\n5z_{1}^{2} - z_{2}^{2} &= 5 \\\\\n\\frac{z_{1}^{2}}{1^{2}} - (z_{2}^{2})/((\\sqrt{5})^{2}) &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents a hyperbola with semiaxes $1$ and $\\sqrt5$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186171841 1071 "Which of the following equations define a non-empty set of points in the ![](images/a4/a49d63b985288c12f1f2e6494534c6d69a52b52cb19b15ff716c5893aaaadbf8.png)-plane?\n\n- ![](images/1f/1f9c6caabac759264990b2d2d23f2dd2a0960a721cbf57e0cc2a405ff5d800c6.png)\n- ![](images/c7/c77f3bd2dde7986a670e81dae93439c2a2be22fc736738a90130b3a82d8027e5.png)\n- ![](images/4e/4e6827d9bc7fcfbcc59953380c1ddbefbf1537adaf4b539c2286338cd001fc06.png)" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186171841 1071 "Which of the following equations define a non-empty set of points in the $x_1x_2$-plane?\n\n1. $x_1^2 +y_2^2= 0$\n2. $x_1^2+\\dfrac{x_2^2}{2} = -1$\n3. $2x_2^2 -x_1^2= 0$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186171841 1074 "Let's examine 