e of quadratic curve is defined by the following equation?\n\n$$\n3x_1^2 + 2x_1 - x_2 =\\dfrac{5}{3}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186171842 1074 "First, we complete the square for the variable $x_1\\mathbin{:}$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 3({x}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{1})-{x}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 3({x}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{1}+{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2})-{x}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 3{({x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-3{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-{x}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 3{({x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}-{x}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 3{({x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} & ={x}_{2}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}={x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}+2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}_{1}^{2} & ={y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents a parabola that opens in the direction of the positive $y_2$-axis." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186171842 1074 "First, we complete the squares for the variables $x_1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x_{1}^{2} + 2x_{1} - x_{2} &= \\frac{5}{3} \\\\\n3(x_{1}^{2} + \\frac{2}{3}x_{1}) - x_{2} &= \\frac{5}{3} \\\\\n3(x_{1}^{2} + \\frac{2}{3}x_{1} + (\\frac{1}{3})^{2} - (\\frac{1}{3})^{2}) - x_{2} &= \\frac{5}{3} \\\\\n3(x_{1} + \\frac{1}{3})^{2} - 3(\\frac{1}{3})^{2} - x_{2} &= \\frac{5}{3} \\\\\n3(x_{1} + \\frac{1}{3})^{2} - \\frac{1}{3} - x_{2} &= \\frac{5}{3} \\\\\n3(x_{1} + \\frac{1}{3})^{2} &= x_{2} + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n{y_{1} = x_{1} + \\frac{1}{3},; y_{2} = x_{2} + 2\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3y_{1}^{2} &= y_{2} \\\\\ny_{1}^{2} &= \\frac{1}{3}y_{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents a parabola that opens in the direction of the positive $y_2$-axis." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186172034 1038 #ed