he equation ${x}_{2}^{2}=-7$ defines an empty set. Notice that the left-hand side is non-negative for all ${x}_{2},$ while the right-hand side is negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186175725 1071 "Consider the standard matrices of three linear transformations shown below. Which of these transformations map $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto a line?\n\n![](images/7c/7cf595d0c2334cd4698c29991e58184b9e33db3f65bb1e99e5ce0c54714a67b3.png)" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186175725 1071 "Consider the standard matrices of three linear transformations shown below. Which of these transformations map $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto a line?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186175725 1074 "Given a linear transformation over $\\mathbb{R}^2$ with the standard matrix $T$, we have the following:\n\n- $T$ maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto $\\mathbb{R}^2$ if and only if $\\text{rank}(T)=2$, i.e., $T$ is nonsingular.\n- $T$ maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto a line if and only if $\\text{rank}(T)=1$.\n- $T$ maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto $(0,0)$ if and only if $\\text{rank}(T)=0$, i.e., $T$ is the zero matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $T_1$ maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto a line. Notice that the columns of $T_1$ are linearly dependent but it's not the zero matrix. So, $\\text{rank}(T_1)=1$.\n- $T_2$ maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto $\\mathbb{R}^2$. Indeed, $T_2$ is a nonsingular matrix, i.e. $\\text{rank}(T_2)=2$.\n- $T_3$ maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto $(0,0)$ since $T_3$ is the zero matrix, i.e. $\\text{rank}(T_3)=0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$T_1$ only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186175725 1074 "Given a linear transformation over $\\mathbb{R}^2$ with the standard matrix $T,$ we have the following:\n\n- $T$ maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto $\\mathbb{R}^2$ if and only if $\\text{rank}(T)=2,$ i.e., $T$ is nonsingular.\n\n- $T$ maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto a line if and only if $\\text{rank}(T)=1.$\n\n- $T$ maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto $(0,0)$ if and only if $\\text{rank}(T)=0,$ i.e., $T$ is the zero matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $T_1$ maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto a line. Notice that the columns of $T_1$ are linearly dependent but it's not the zero matrix. So, $\\text{rank}(T_1)=1.$\n\n- $T_2$ maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Indeed, $T_2$ is a nonsingular matrix, i.e. $\\text{rank}(T_2)=2.$\n\n- $T_3$ maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto $(0,0)$ since $T_3$ is the zero matrix, i.e. $\\text{rank}(T_3)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$T_1$ only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186175726 1071 "Consider the standard matrix $T$ of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -9 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nFind the equation of the line that the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186175726 1071 "Consider the standard matrix $T$ of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by\n![](images/7b/7b576374332b6a30e024643ca3398b1c04c742a9994346e01c1b1b99495e4677.png)\nFind the equation of the line that the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186175726 1074 "Notice that $T$ has proportional rows, yet $T$ is not the zero matrix.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T})=1\n$$\n\nand $\\mathbf{T}$ maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto a line that passes through the origin.\n\nMoreover, the line must pass through the origin $(0,0)$ and the points $(6,2)$ and $(-9,-3)$, which are defined by the columns of $T$.\n\nComputing the slope $m$ of this line, we get\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{-3-2}{-9-6} = \\dfrac13\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf{T}$ maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto the line\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac13x\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186175726 1074 "Notice that $T$ has proportional rows, yet $T$ is not the zero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T})=1$ and $\\mathbf{T}$ maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto a line that passes through the origin.\n\nMoreover, the line must pass through the origin $(0,0)$ and the points $(6,2)$ and $(-9,-3),$ which are defined by the columns of $T.$ \n\nComputing the slope $m$ of this line, we get\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{-3-2}{-9-6} = \\dfrac13.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf{T}$ maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto the line $y=\\dfrac13x.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186175727 1071 "Consider a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ that maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto the line $y=-5x$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\langle -1, 5 \\rangle \\in \\text{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$\n2. $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T})=\\{0\\}$\n3. $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\Bbb R^2$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186175727 1071 "Consider a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ that maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto the line $y=-5x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\langle -1, 5 \\rangle \\in \\text{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$\n\n- $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T})=\\{0\\}$\n\n- $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\Bbb R^2$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186175727 1074 "Recall that when the image of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ with the standard matrix $T$ is a one-dimensional line, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(T) = \\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) = 1\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nnullity(T) = nullity(T) &= 2 - rank(T) \\\\\n&= 2 - 1 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the point $(-1,5)$ satisfies the equation of the line $y=-5x$ since $5=-5\\cdot (-1)$. Hence,\n$\\langle -1, 5 \\rangle \\in \\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$.\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\textrm{nullity}(T)=1$, we get\n$\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\{\\mathbf0\\}$.\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T})=1$, we get\n$\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\Bbb R^2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186175727 1074 "Recall that when the image of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ with the standard matrix $T$ is a one-dimensional line, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(T) = \\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) = 1,\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the point $(-1,5)$ satisfies the equation of the line $y=-5x$ since $5=-5\\cdot (-1).$ Hence, \n\n$$\n\\langle -1, 5 \\rangle \\in \\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}).\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\textrm{nullity}(T)=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\{\\mathbf0\\}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false.  Since $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T})=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\Bbb R^2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186175826 1071 "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t,z) = \\big\\langle \\text{_____}, \\: 4\\sin{t}, \\: z \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t,z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\dfrac{x^2}{4}+\\dfrac{y^2}{16}=1.$ What is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186175826 1071 "$\\mathbf{f}(t,z) = \\big\\langle \\text{_____}, \\: 4\\sin{t}, \\: z \\big\\rangle$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t,z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\dfrac{x^2}{4}+\\dfrac{y^2}{16}=1$. What is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$?" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186175826 1074 "Recall that the ellipse\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{4}+\\dfrac{y^2}{16}=1\n$$\n\nin $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n\\langle x,y \\rangle = \\big\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 4\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad t \\in [0,2\\pi)\n$$\n\nsince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2}}{4} + \\frac{y^{2}}{16} &= ((2\\cos t)^{2})/(4) + ((4\\sin t)^{2})/(16) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4\\cos^{2} t}{4} + \\frac{16\\sin^{2} t}{16} \\\\\n&= \\cos^{2} t + \\sin^{2} t \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(t, z) &= ⟨x, y, z⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨2\\cos t, 4\\sin t, z⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in [0,2\\pi)$ and $z \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}2\\cos{t}}$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186175826 1074 "Recall that the ellipse $\\dfrac{x^2}{4}+\\dfrac{y^2}{16}=1$ in $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n\\langle x,y \\rangle = \\big\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 4\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle,\n\\qquad t \\in [0,2\\pi),\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{cos}^{2}⁡t}{4}+\\frac{16{sin}^{2}⁡t}{16} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in [0,2\\pi)$ and $z \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}2\\cos{t}}.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186175828 1071 "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(x,y) = \\big\\langle x, \\: y, \\: \\text{___} \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ above parametrizes the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $2x^2=z.$ What is the third component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186175828 1071 "$\\mathbf{f}(x,y) = \\big\\langle x, \\: y, \\: \\text{___} \\big\\rangle$\n\nThe v