me{tan}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =9({sec}^{2}⁡t-{tan}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186176271 1068 #edb/ref 17592186173854 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186176271 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362591 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186176271 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186176271 1071 "The random variable $X$ has the following probability distribution.\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ |\n\nLet $Y=2X+1.$ The distribution of $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $y$ | $-3$ | $a$ | $1$ | $b$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $c$ | $0.2$ | $d$ |\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186176271 1071 "The random variable $X$ has the following probability distribution.\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f_{X}(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ |\n\nLet $Y = 2X + 1$. The distribution of $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $y$ | $-3$ | $a$ | $1$ | $b$ | $5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f_{Y}(y)$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $c$ | $0.2$ | $d$ |\n\nWhat is the value of $a + b + c$?" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186176271 1074 "First, let's add a $Y=2X+1$ row to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nNow, ${f}_{X}(x)={f}_{Y}(y)$ for corresponding values of $X$ and $Y.$ Therefore, the distribution of $Y$ is as follows:\n\n| $y$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ |\n\nSo, we have $a=-1,b=3,c=0.1,$ and therefore $a+b+c=2.1.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186176275 1068 #edb/ref 17592186173878 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186176275 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362597 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186176275 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186176275 1071 "Consider the standard matrices of three linear transformations shown below. Which of the transformations map $R^{2}$ onto $(0, 0)$?\n\n$T_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, T_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}, T_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186176275 1071 "Consider the standard matrices of three linear transformations shown below. Which of the transformations map ${R}^{2}$ onto $(0,0)?$\n\n$$\n{T}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{T}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{T}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186176275 1074 "Given a linear transformation over ${R}^{2}$ with the standard matrix $T,$ we have the following:\n\n- $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto ${R}^{2}$ if and only if $\\text{rank}(T)=2,$ i.e., $T$ is nonsingular.\n\n- $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line if and only if $\\text{rank}(T)=1.$\n\n- $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto $(0,0)$ if and only if $\\text{rank}(T)=0,$ i.e., $T$ is the zero matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- ${T}_{1}$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line. Notice that ${T}_{1}$ has a zero column but it's not the zero matrix. So, $\\text{rank}({T}_{1})=1.$\n\n- ${T}_{2}$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line. Notice that the columns of ${T}_{2}$ are linearly dependent but it's not the zero matrix. So, $\\text{rank}({T}_{2})=1.$\n\n- ${T}_{3}$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto $(0,0)$ since ${T}_{3}$ is the zero matrix, i.e. $\\text{rank}({T}_{3})=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${T}_{3}$ only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186176418 1068 #edb/ref 17592186174726 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186176418 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363254 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186176418 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186176418 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=2t-4,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  The positive direction is left to right. If the particle has velocity $v=12\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=6\\,\\text{s},$ determine the time intervals in which the particle moves to the left." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186176418 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $m/s^{2}$, given by the function $a(t) = 2t - 4$, where $t > 0$ is the time in seconds. The positive direction is left to right. If the particle has velocity $v = 12m/s$ when $t = 6s$, determine the time intervals in which the particle moves to the left." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186176418 1074 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2t-4)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}-4t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(6)=12.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =(6)^{2}-4(6)+C \\\\ 12 & =12+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{2}-4t.$\n\nTo find the time interval in which the particle is moving to the *left*, we must solve the inequality $v<0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}-4t=t(t-4)<0.\n$$\n\nPlotting the graph of $v(t)$, we get the following picture.\n\n![](images/4c/4ceab0aa50ce1a1fa8a49755ccc4f33a159cf5081be7ed86fe78d1453e2b5298.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $v<0$ whenever $0<t<4.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186176482 1068 #edb/ref 17592186175000 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186176482 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363853 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186176482 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186176482 1071 "Consider a linear transformation $T$ that maps $R^{2}$ to the origin. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $rank(T) = 1$, where $T$ is the standard matrix of $T$\n2. $Im(T)$ contains no vectors of $R^{2}$\n3. $Ker(T) = R^{2}$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186176482 1071 "Consider a linear transformation $T$ that maps ${R}^{2}$ to the origin. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{rank}(T)=1$,  where $T$ is the standard matrix of $T$\n- $\\text{Im}(T)$ contains no vectors of ${R}^{2}$\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)={R}^{2}$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186176482 1074 "Recall that when the image of a linear transformation $T$ in ${R}^{2}$ with the standard matrix $T$ is the zero-subspace ${0},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=0\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. In our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The image of $T$ contains the vector zero vector $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the condition $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$ in ${R}^{2}$ is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\text{Ker}(T)={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186176485 1068 #edb/ref 17592186175018 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186176485 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363873 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186176485 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186176485 1071 "Let $v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}a_{1} \\\\ b_{1}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}a_{2} \\\\ b_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$.\nGiven that $λ_{1} = 2\\textrm{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A$, find the value of $\\frac{a_{1}a_{2}}{b_{1}b_{2}}$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186176485 1071 "Let ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}].$\nGiven that ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186176485 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2-2\\text{i} & 4 \\\\ -2 & -2-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(2-2\\text{i}){x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+(-2-2\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-2\\text{i}I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nOn the other hand, since ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the  complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}=\\overset{―}{{\\lambda }_{1}}=-2\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector of ${\\lambda }_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}=\\frac{(-2)(-2)}{(1-\\text{i})(1+\\text{i})}=2.\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186176510 1068 #edb/ref 17592186175168 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186176510 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363972 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186176510 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186176510 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=3-3{t}^{2},$ where $t⩾0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=2\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=2\\,\\text{s},$ calculate the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}$ of the particle and determine the time whan the particle reaches its maximum velocity." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186176510 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $m/s^{2}$, given by the function $a(t) = 3 - 3t^{2}$, where $t⩾0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v = 2m/s$ when $t = 2s$, calculate the maximum velocity $v_{max}$ of the particle and determine the time whan the particle reaches its maximum velocity." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186176510 1074 "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t)=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 3-3{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3(1-t)(1+t) & =0 \\\\ t & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t⩾0$, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $t=1\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the second derivative test. For this, we find the second derivative of $v(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{″}(t) & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3-3{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =-6t \\\\ {v}^{″}(1) & =-6(1) \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${v}^{″}(1)<0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=1\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3-3{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3t-{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(2)=2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(2)-{2}^{3}+C & =2 \\\\ 6-8+C & =2\\,⟹\\,C=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=4+3t-{t}^{3}.$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{\\text{max}}=v(1)=4+3(1)-{1}^{3}=6\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186179557 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x \\geq 1$. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,\\rm{m/s}$, the magnitude of its acceleration is $(2x-2) \\, \\rm{m/s}^2$, and the acceleration is directed toward $O$.\nIf $P$ has velocity $\\sqrt2\\,\\rm{m/s}$ when its displacement is $1\\,\\rm{m}$, find the value of $x$ for which $P$ is instantaneously at rest." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186179557 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x \\geq 1.$  When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,\\rm{m/s},$ the magnitude of its acceleration is $(2x-2) \\, \\rm{m/s}^2,$ and the acceleration is directed toward $O.$\nIf $P$ has velocity $\\sqrt2\\,\\rm{m/s}$ when its displacement is $1\\,\\rm{m},$ find the value of $x$ for which $P$ is instantaneously at rest." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186179557 1074 "![](images/0c/0c47a1e988a75e4813c92a76acbf3a821364995123f3eb394b07d4bfaa95f80d.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $(2x-2)\\,\\rm{m/s}^2$ and is directed *toward* $O$, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} = -(2x-2)\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t}= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right)\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right) = -(2x-2)\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} \\left(\\dfrac12v^2\\right) \\textrm{d}x = \\int (-2x+2) \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12v^2 = -x^2+2x + C\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nv=\\sqrt{2}\\,\\rm{m/s}\n$$\n\nwhen\n\n$$\nx=1\\,\\rm{m}\n$$\n\nwe can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}v^{2} &= -x^{2} + 2x + C \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{2})^{2} &= - (1)^{2} + 2(1) + C \\\\\nC &= 1 + 1 - 2 \\\\\nC &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12v^2 = -x^2+2x\n$$\n\nFinally, the particle is instantaneously at rest when\n\n$$\nv(x) = 0\\,\\rm{m/s}\n$