 1081 "\\frac{4}{15}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362553 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362553 1084 #uuid "c1f0bc05-f230-5683-8d64-36ab56eaf892" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362554 1081 "\\frac{6}{125}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362554 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362554 1084 #uuid "272b7f96-81c1-553a-ad34-6eb75ca94ac2" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362555 1081 "\\frac{1}{25}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362555 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362555 1084 #uuid "50b79a59-5b67-5182-ad29-ebc1c24bf463" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362556 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362557 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362556 1069 "q-109331" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362556 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362556 1071 "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\sqrt{y-3}$ that lies between the lines $y=3$ and $y=\\frac{15}{4}.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362556 1072 #uuid "4b0f3d46-2a1f-5dae-8237-79287f94cce2" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362556 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\sqrt{y-3})=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y-3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4(y-3)}.\n$$\n\nThen, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{3}^{15/4}\\sqrt{y-3}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4(y-3)}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{3}^{15/4}\\sqrt{y-3}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{4y-11}{4(y-3)}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{3}^{15/4}\\sqrt{4y-11}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=4y-11.$ Then, $\\text{d}y=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.$\n\nWe also need to compute new limits:\n\n| **$y$** | $3$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =\\pi {\\int }_{3}^{15/4}\\sqrt{4y-11}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot {[\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(\\sqrt{{4}^{3}}-\\sqrt{{1}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(8-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362557 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362558 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362557 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362559 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362557 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362560 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362557 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362561 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362557 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362562 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362557 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362557 1077 "selection" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362557 1079 #edb/ref 17592186362561 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362557 1080 #uuid "bf4dec28-6ad5-5a64-90ef-6978e1019e65" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362558 1081 "12\\pi " 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362558 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585