rmula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-4x & =0 \\\\ -x({x}^{2}-4x+4) & =0 \\\\ -x(x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-4x)=-3{x}^{2}+8x-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\sqrt{1+{(-3{x}^{2}+8x-4)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362564 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362565 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362564 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362566 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362564 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362567 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362564 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362568 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362564 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362569 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362564 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362564 1077 "selection" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362564 1079 #edb/ref 17592186362565 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362564 1080 #uuid "1cf3c163-585a-58e8-bdd3-011ef184838b" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362565 1081 "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\sqrt{1+{(-3{x}^{2}+8x-4)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186