}^{′}(t),$ and $p$ and $q$ are constants. Calculate  $(p-q)\\cdot f(t).$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362570 1072 #uuid "1d2e9b0a-6339-50e4-8938-2c9567f7a9d0" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362570 1074 "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also convert the initial conditions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & ={x}_{1}(1)=1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(1) & ={x}_{2}(1)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=-\\frac{5}{12}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{6}y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=-\\frac{5}{12}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(1)=1 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{1}-\\frac{5}{12}{x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{2}(1)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $p=1,q=-2,f(t)=\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{1}-\\frac{5}{12}{x}_{2},$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(p-q)\\cdot f(t) & =(1-(-2))(\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{1}-\\frac{5}{12}{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{1}-\\frac{5}{12}{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{1}-\\frac{5}{4}{x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362571 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362572 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362571 1075 #edb/ref 1759