ambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-2x,-2y,2z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,-1,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2x=2\\lambda x \\\\ -2y=-\\lambda  \\\\ 2z=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}(1+\\lambda )x=0 \\\\ y=\\frac{\\lambda }{2} \\\\ (1-\\lambda )z=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- First, assume that $\\lambda \\ne \\pm 1.$ Then, by the first and third equations, we must have $x=z=0.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\ny={0}^{2}+{0}^{2}\\,⇒\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(0,0,0).$\n\n- Next, assume that $\\lambda =-1.$ Then, by the second and third equations, we must have $y=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $z=0,$ but this contradicts the constraint $g(x,y,z)=0.$\n\n- Finally, assume that $\\lambda =1.$ Then, by the first and second equations, we must have $x=0$ and $y=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}={0}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\,⇒\\,z=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}).$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(0,0,0)\\,$ | $\\,(0,\\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,0\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at the points $(0,\\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}),$ and its maximum value is $\\frac{1}{4}.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363034 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363035 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363034 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363036 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363034 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363037 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363034 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363038 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363034 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363039 13194139533445 175921