 and the acceleration is directed toward $O.$ If $P$ has velocity $v=20\\,m/s$ at the origin and the particle is at rest when its displacement is $10\\,m,$ find the value of $k.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363053 1072 #uuid "89d922d3-34d3-536f-bab1-5d011a22c471" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363053 1074 "![](images/cd/cd42eb74e5bba02951118c349a1b25f7193c39aa53f6504403918dfa984eb2c2.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $kx\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-kx.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-kx.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (-kx)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=-\\frac{k}{2}{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=20\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =-\\frac{k}{2}{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(20)^{2} & =-\\frac{k}{2}(0)^{3}+C \\\\ C & =200\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=200-\\frac{k}{2}{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $v=0\\,m/s$ when $x=10\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =200-\\frac{k}{2}{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2} & =200-\\frac{k}{2}(10)^{2} \\\\ 50k & =200 \\\\ k & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363054 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363055 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363054 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363056 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363054 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363057 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363054 1075 #edb/ref 1759218636305