ficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-6\\alpha =12\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha -6\\beta =8\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-2,\\,\\beta =-\\frac{5}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2x-\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{6x}-2x-\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{6(0)}-2(0)-\\frac{5}{3} & =2 \\\\ A-\\frac{5}{3} & =2 \\\\ A & =\\frac{11}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{11}{3}\\,{e}^{6x}-2x-\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363435 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363436 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363435 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363435 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363435 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363436 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363435 1080 #uuid "9c5af851-8b99-5ee8-ab29-3944df50aa73" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363436 1081 "\\frac{11}{3}{e}^{6x}-2x-\\frac{5}{3}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363436 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363436 1084 #uuid "b6311dbc-76a3-5ea5-8492-1ac8994a78ce" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363437 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363438 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363437 1069 "q-333058" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363437 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363437 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+y=3,\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+y=3\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363437 1072 #uuid "3b4fb91c-5bf2-54eb-af95-268e2d101d2f" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363437 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a constant. Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a constant, i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\beta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\beta $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\beta =3\n$$\n\nSolving for $\\beta $ gives $\\beta =3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-x}+3.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{0}+3 & =-1 \\\\ A+3 & =-1 \\\\ A & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-4\\,{e}^{-x}+3.\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363438 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363439 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [175921