n the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{6x+4yz}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is\n$z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=0.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=0, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{6x+4yz}{{x}^{2}},\\, & z(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y$ and ${y}^{′}=z$ at $x=\\frac{5}{4},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5}{4}-1=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=0 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{6x+4yz}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 0\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |  | $0$ | $6$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =0 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $6$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+0=0 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=0+\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding