\\ 3{y}^{″} & ={x}^{2}-2{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-2{y}^{′}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{2}-2z}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=0.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=2, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{2}-2z}{3},\\, & z(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z={y}^{′}$ at $x=1$ and asked to find their values at $x=3.$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=3$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-1}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=2$ and $z(1)=0.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=0 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-2z}{3}=\\frac{{1}^{2}-2\\cdot 0}{3}=\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 1=\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+0=2 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=0+\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ |  |  | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe com