ting on the object is the force due to gravity." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363524 1072 #uuid "5ccbff46-9435-57a4-ae53-244098c20951" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363524 1074 "Let $R$ be the planet's radius and $g$ be the planet's surface gravitational acceleration.\n\nAccording to Newton's law of universal gravitation, the magnitude of the inward gravitational force pulling the object toward the planet is\n\n$$\n{F}_{\\text{in}}=G\\,\\frac{mM}{{r}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $G$ is the gravitational constant, $m$ is the object's mass, and $M$ is the planet's mass.\n\nSince this is the only force and it acts inward, the resultant force, taking *outward* as positive, is $F=-{F}_{\\text{in}}.$\nApplying Newton's second law, $F=ma,$ where $a$ is the acceleration of the object, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =-{F}_{\\text{in}} \\\\ ma & =-G\\,\\frac{mM}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ a & =-\\frac{GM}{{r}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the acceleration is inversely proportional to the square of the distance, \n\n$$\na=-\\frac{k}{{r}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwith constant of proportionality $k=GM>0.$\n\nWhen at the surface, $r=R,$ the acceleration of the object due to gravity is $a=-g.$ Substituting into our previous equation, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n-g=-\\frac{k}{{R}^{2}}\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,k=g{R}^{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting back into the equation, we get\n\n$$\na=-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, since acceleration $a$ is the derivative of velocity $v,$ which is the derivative of distance $r,$ we can express acceleration as follows using the chain rule, treating $v$ as a function of $r$ along the trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\  & =\\frac{dr}{dt}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dr} \\\\  & =v\\frac{dv}{dr}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result into our equation, we obtain a separable differential equation for the velocity:\n\n$$\nv\\frac{dv}{dr}=-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSolving using separation of variables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int v\\frac{dv}{dr}\\,dr & =\\int (-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}})\\,dr \\\\ \\int v\\,dv & =-g{R}^{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{r}^{2}}\\,dr \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}}{2} & =-g{R}^{2}(-\\frac{1}{r})+{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{r}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C=2{C}_{1}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nLet ${v}_{0}$ be the initial velocity of the object. Applying the initial condition $v(R)={v}_{0},$ we can solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(R)^{2} & =\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{R}+C \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =2gR+C \\\\ C & ={v}_{0}^{2}-2gR\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation for the velocity of the object is\n\n$$\n{v}^{2}=\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{r}+{v}_{0}^{2}-2gR.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting in the values\n$R=(1.7\\times {10}^{7}),$\n$g=11.8,$ and\n${v}_{0}=(7.4\\times {10}^{4}),$\nwe conclude that the equation for the velocity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{2\\cdot 11.8\\cdot (1.7\\times {10}^{7})^{2}}{r}+(7.4\\times {10}^{4})^{2}-2\\cdot 11.8\\cdot (1.7\\times {10}^{7}) \\\\  & =\\frac{23.6\\cdot (2.89\\times {10}^{14})}{r}+(54.76\\times {10}^{8})-(40.12\\times {10}^{7}) \\\\  & =\\frac{68.204\\times {10}^{14}}{r}+(54.76\\times {10}^{8})-(4.012\\times {10}^{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6.8204\\times {10}^{15}}{r}+((54.76-4.012)\\times {10}^{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6.8204\\times {10}^{15}}{r}+(50.748\\times {10}^{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6.8204\\times {10}^{15}}{r}+(5.0748\\times {10}^{9}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363525 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363526 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363525 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363527 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363525 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363528 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363525 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363529 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363525 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363530 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363525 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363525 1077 "selection" 1319413