}x).\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we apply the initial conditions. Using $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+0+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ because the particular solution is a sine series.\n\nNext, we use the derivative condition ${y}^{′}(0)=-5.$ First, we compute the derivative of the total solution (with $A=0$):\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)∼B\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n}{2}{b}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n}{2}x)\n$$\n\nApplying ${y}^{′}(0)=-5$ and noting that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5 & =B\\sqrt{3}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n}{2}{b}_{n} \\\\ B\\sqrt{3} & =-5-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n}{2}{b}_{n} \\\\ B & =-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{3}}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n{b}_{n}}{2\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $B$ back into ${y}_{c}=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x),$ we find the missing part:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)(-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{3}}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n{b}_{n}}{2\\sqrt{3}})+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{n}{2}x)\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363756 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363757 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363756 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363758 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363756 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363759 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363756 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363760 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363756 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363756 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363756 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363759 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363756 1080 #uuid "7b5e0295-3d2a-5ea3-b1a9-dd01a18b1b65" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363757 1081 "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)(-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{3}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n{b}_{n}}{\