82e40107" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363761 1074 "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(x)$ is given by $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},$ where ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we address the particular solution ${y}_{p}.$ Since $f(x)$ is an even function and the differential operator contains no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits the symmetry and is also an even function consisting only of cosine terms. This is represented by the term\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nNext, we find the homogeneous solution. The characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{2}+8=0$ gives roots $\\lambda =\\pm \\sqrt{8}\\text{i}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x).\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we apply the initial conditions. Differentiating the total solution gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)∼-A\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)+B\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =-A\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+B\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\ 0 & =0+B\\sqrt{8}-0 \\\\ B & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because the total solution is an even function, its derivative is an odd function, which must vanish at $x=0.$\n\nNext, we use the condition $y(0)=-2.$ With $B=0,$ the total solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nApplying $y(0)=-2$ and noting that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =A+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} \\\\ A & =-2-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A$ back into ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x),$ we find the missing part:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)(-2-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n})+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363762 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363763 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363762 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363764 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363762 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363765 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363762 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363766 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363762 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363762 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363762 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363763 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363762 1080 #uuid "9ab9d6fd-f448-55e9-b96d-b8b72364ed81" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363763 1081 "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)(-2-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n})" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363763 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363763 1084 #uuid "c43daebb-d90e-5f56-8f36-6c2339f67f42" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363764 1081 "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+2)" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363764 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363764 1084 #uuid "abb08875-6849-5046-819a-ba8c196e97ed" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363765 1081 "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)(-2+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n})" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363765 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363765 1084 #uuid "3639c708-e27d-5c89-b671-db99d9bc675a" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363766 1081 "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)(-2-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n})" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363766 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363766 1084 #uuid "730c2a39-24f7-5e69-8ad1-ee3baab0c9b6" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363767 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363768 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363767 1069 "q-344047" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363767 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363767 1071 "$$\n{y}^{″}+4{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x),\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above, where $f(x)$ is an odd, $6$-periodic function. Select the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the second infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{36(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{3}(36-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363767 1072 #uuid "c618b807-0d3f-5c96-b840-87c2666fd829" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363767 1074 "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(x)$ is given by $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},$ where ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we address the particular solution ${y}_{p}.$ Since $f(x)$ is an odd function, its Fourier series consists only of sine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an odd function. This is represented by the term\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{36(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{3}(36-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNext, we find the homogeneous solution. The characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{2}+4{\\pi }^{2}=0$ gives roots $\\lambda =\\pm 2\\pi \\text{i}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x).\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we apply the initial conditions. Using $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+0+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ because the particular solution is a sine series.\n\nNext, we use the derivative condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$ First, we compute the derivative of the total solution (with $A=0$):\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)∼B(2\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(36-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$\n\nApplying ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ and noting that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =B(2\\pi )+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(36-{n}^{2})} \\\\ B(2\\pi ) & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(36-{n}^{2})} \\\\ B & =-\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(36-{n}^{2})} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{2n{\\pi }^{3}(36-{n}^{2})} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(36-{n}^{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $B$ back into ${y}_{c}=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)$, we find the missing part:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(36-{n}^{2})}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{36(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{3}(36-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363768 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363769 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363768 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363770 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363768 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363771 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363768 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363772 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363768 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363768 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363768 10