)=2t-5,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  The positive direction is left to right. If the train has velocity $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=5\\,\\text{s},$ determine the time interval in which the train moves to the left.\n\n{{field-1}}\n$<t<$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363773 1072 #uuid "6916e525-178d-5e14-99f4-1c9df87974e0" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363773 1074 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2t-5)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}-5t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(5)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(5)^{2}-5(5)+C \\\\ 0 & =25-25+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the train at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{2}-5t.$\n\nTo find the time interval in which the train is moving to the *left*, we must solve the inequality $v<0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}-5t=t(t-5)<0.\n$$\n\nPlotting the graph of $v(t)$, we get the following picture.\n\n![](images/af/afe458cdaf5eedbfb78cd9067b0c8caa209535391156bb5de14de34562f3cc1c.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $v<0$ whenever  $0\\,<\\,t<5.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363774 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363775 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363774 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363774 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363774 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363775 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363774 1080 #uuid "b67b2d9b-211f-5c47-bad5-b506e027b04c" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363775 1081 "0" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363775 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363775 1084 #uuid "b61a3efe-9fc2-5947-8f09-21fd105ac642" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363776 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363777 1319413953