592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364033 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=(2t+1)^{3}$, where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=\\frac{65}{8}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s}$, determine the moment in time when the particle is stationary." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364033 1072 #uuid "b219724a-8fbc-5643-88ad-38f4f5993b95" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364033 1074 "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2t+1)^{3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(2t+1)^{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=\\frac{65}{8}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{65}{8} & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot {[2(1)+1]}^{4}+C \\\\ \\frac{65}{8} & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot (81)+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{65}{8}-\\frac{81}{8} \\\\ C & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{1}{8}(2t+1)^{4}-2.$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}(2t+1)^{4}-2 & =0 \\\\ (2t+1)^{4} & =16 \\\\ 2t+1 & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $t=-\\frac{3}{2}\\,,\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nSince $t>0$, we discard solution $t=-\\frac{3}{2}.$ \nTherefore, the particle is stationary when $t=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364034 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364035 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364034 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364036 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364034 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364037 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364034 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364038 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17