92186045585 true] [17592186364171 1081 "-2" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364171 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364171 1084 #uuid "c03c8cfa-dd96-52a1-98a2-b3436e3bfde5" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364172 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364173 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364172 1069 "q-80800" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364172 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364172 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -14\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n- $\\lambda =-14$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n- The eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ has algebraic multiplicity $2$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364172 1072 #uuid "c6753a04-a08e-5bc2-a11a-2cad03fb8d3f" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364172 1074 "First, we find the matrix $\\,P-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nP-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -3 & -8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -\\lambda  & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2-\\lambda  & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -14-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(P-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nNotice that we can expand the $4\\times 4$ determinant across the $1$st column, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -3 & -8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -\\lambda  & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2-\\lambda  & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -14-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & 0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -2-\\lambda  & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -14-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the $3\\times 3$ determinant across the $3$rd row, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2-\\lambda )\\cdot (-14-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(-14-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda +14)\\lambda (\\lambda +2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $P$ are $\\lambda =2,$ $\\lambda =-2,$ $\\lambda =0,$ and $\\lambda =-14$  (all with algebraic multiplicity $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364173 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364174 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364173 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364175 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364173 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364176 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364173 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364177 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364173 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364178 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364173 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364173 1077 "selection" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364173 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364177 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364173 1080 #uuid "abafa309-3832-58cf-8a1e-a1f4d098470e" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364174 1081 "I, II, and III" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364174 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364174 1084 #uuid "27af9af5-40f7-5590-87de-4650f27e5aa7" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364175 1081 "II and III only" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364175 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364175 1084 #uuid "a879185a-10d3-51bd-a415-70db009b2238" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364176 1081 "I and III only" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364176 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364176 1084 #uuid "9f7d7228-7d99-5e51-bc6b-b09cd75c534e" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364177 1081 "I and II only" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364177 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364177 1084 #uuid "152a4a84-a477-515b-8d42-be94175385d3" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364178 1081 "II only" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364178 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364178 1084 #uuid "c815e277-06a0-5dd2-ba04-f1aa13bc716f" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364179 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364180 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364179 1069 "q-81041" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364179 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364179 1071 "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n$f(x,y)$ \ngiven that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4x & 2{y}^{2}-2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364179 1072 #uuid "16f8ddbb-3563-5450-8b85-07ba89da4f50" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364179 1074 "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4x & 2{y}^{2}-2\\end{aligned}],$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=4x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{y}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4x=0 \\\\ 2{y}^{2}=2\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,1)$ and $(0,-1)$ are the stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x)=4\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{y}^{2}-2)=0 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{2}-2)=4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,1)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,1),$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(H(0,1))=4\\cdot 4-{0}^{2}=16>0.\n$$\n\nNow, since $H(0,1)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,1)=({f}^{″}(0,1))_{11}=4>0,$ the function $f$ has a local minimum at $(0,1).$\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,-1)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $H(0,-1),$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(H(-1,0))=4\\cdot (-4)-{0}^{2}=-16<0\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(0,-1)$ is saddle point.\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,-1)$ and a local minimum at $(0,1).$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364180 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364181 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364180 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364182 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364180 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364183 13194139533445 175921860455