xt{s}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364275 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364275 1084 #uuid "e5924bdc-2994-5f8f-a876-a40590f107d6" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364276 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364277 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364276 1069 "q-84225" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364276 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364276 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)={t}^{1/3}-1,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=4\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=8\\,\\text{s},$ determine the moment in time when the particle is stationary." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364276 1072 #uuid "a54d954d-f678-50a4-85cb-fbb21be9de6f" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364276 1074 "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ({t}^{1/3}-1)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{4/3}-t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(8)=4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =\\frac{3}{4}(8)^{4/3}-8+C \\\\ 4 & =12-8+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{4/3}-t.$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{4/3}-t & =0 \\\\ t(\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{1/3}-1) & =0 \\\\ t & =0,\\frac{64}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t>0,$ we discard the solution $t=0.$ Therefore, the particle is stationary when $t=\\frac{64}{27}\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364277 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364278 13194139533445 1759218604558